<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Haptics, and only for two of the six patterns.

      Four of them play through `View.performHapticFeedback`, which needs no
      permission at all. `success` and `error` have no HapticFeedbackConstants
      equivalent — nothing in that vocabulary means "the thing you asked for
      finished" — so those two go through Vibrator, and this is what for.

      Install-time, and it does not prompt. `Haptics.kt` tags every Vibrator
      call with a touch usage so the OS still applies the owner's haptics
      setting to it; without that the permission would buy the ability to
      ignore them, which is not what it is here for.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      Hosting while the app is not in front of the user.

      None of these three prompts. They are install-time permissions, granted
      by having been declared — there is no "allow this app to run in the
      background" dialog on Android for a foreground service. What reviews
      whether the use is legitimate is Play, not the OS.

      WAKE_LOCK is not implied by the service and is not redundant: a
      foreground service stops the *process* being reclaimed, it does not stop
      the CPU suspending once the screen goes off.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />

    <!--
      The one that does prompt, from API 33. It gates the *notification*, not
      the service: denied, hosting still runs at foreground priority and the
      player simply gets no indicator and no Stop button. Requested when the
      first server is started rather than at launch, so the ask arrives with an
      obvious reason attached.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      enableOnBackInvokedCallback: the app opts in to predictive back now
      rather than being opted in for it. It becomes the default at targetSdk 36,
      which Play requires by 31 Aug 2026, so the choice is only whether the
      change lands while it can still be tested or on the day the SDK is bumped.
      Until then it also silences the "OnBackInvokedCallback is not enabled"
      warning logcat prints on every launch.

      networkSecurityConfig: everything this app talks to over the network is
      HTTPS, and res/xml/network_security_config.xml states that so the platform
      enforces it instead of it being a property of the code that happens to
      hold today.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".HomerunApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Homerun">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Homerun"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|keyboardHidden|uiMode|density|fontScale">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Invite and join links: homerun://join/<code>,
              homerun://server|play|web-share-join/<id>.

              No android:host is declared, so every host under the scheme
              matches. The UI's parseDeepLink is the authority on which shapes
              are meaningful, and it rejects the rest — filtering here as well
              would mean two places to update when a link type is added.

              Auth does NOT come through here. The magic-link flow polls the
              backend with a client_nonce, so login completes wherever the
              link is opened.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/app_name">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="homerun" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Keeps the app process alive while a server runs or a backup uploads.

          `specialUse` is the honest type for hosting a game server. It is not
          media playback, not a data transfer on the user's behalf in the sense
          the platform means, and not a companion device. `dataSync` would fit
          the backup half and is deliberately not declared: from Android 15 a
          dataSync service is capped at six hours in any 24, which would be a
          limit on how long somebody may play.

          `specialUse` is the type Play reviews by hand, against the subtype
          string below and the declaration in Play Console. Google's own
          guidance is that it "will likely reject apps using specialUse if
          another foreground service type is appropriate" — so the string is
          written to answer that question and not merely to describe the
          feature. It is one line on purpose: XML normalises newlines in an
          attribute value into runs of spaces, and a reviewer should not have
          to read around them.

          That review has not happened yet — see plans/android.md, open
          question 1. If it is refused, the fallback is `dataSync` and the
          six-hour cap comes with it.

          Not exported: nothing outside this app may start it. `stopWithTask`
          is left at its default of false on purpose — swiping the app out of
          Recents does not disconnect the players; the notification's Stop
          does. See HostingService.onTaskRemoved.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".HostingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Runs a Minecraft server on this device so the player's friends can join it, for as long as the player chooses to keep it online, and finishes uploading the world to their backup repository after they stop it. No other foreground service type fits: the work is not media playback, not a phone call, not location, and not a connected or companion device. It is not dataSync either — nothing is being synchronised on the user's behalf; the device is answering a game protocol for other players in real time, and the six-hour daily limit on dataSync would cap how long a player may host." />
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
